WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.467
Chroma.

2
00:00:01.767 --> 00:00:05.300
The fastest path from nothing to a working retrieval system.

3
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:09.467
Chroma is the SQLite of vector databases.

4
00:00:09.767 --> 00:00:12.868
It's embedded, and persists to a local directory.

5
00:00:12.868 --> 00:00:16.033
Documents, embeddings, and metadata live together.

6
00:00:16.333 --> 00:00:19.150
No container, no config, no schema ceremony.

7
00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:21.967
It can even run the embedding model for you.

8
00:00:23.167 --> 00:00:24.433
Here's the shape of it.

9
00:00:24.733 --> 00:00:27.433
A persistent client points at a local folder.

10
00:00:27.733 --> 00:00:31.267
A collection bundles documents, embeddings, and metadata.

11
00:00:31.567 --> 00:00:35.267
You query with text, plus a where filter built into every query.

12
00:00:35.567 --> 00:00:36.967
And you get your top hits.

13
00:00:38.167 --> 00:00:39.833
Here's Chroma in a few lines.

14
00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:42.490
A persistent client and a collection.

15
00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:44.400
The database is just a folder.

16
00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:47.233
We add a document with an id and metadata.

17
00:00:47.533 --> 00:00:49.342
Then query with a where filter.

18
00:00:49.342 --> 00:00:52.900
Kill the process, restart, and the collection is still there.

19
00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:55.533
Three facts to remember.

20
00:00:55.833 --> 00:00:59.600
Its form factor is an embedded library, installed with pip.

21
00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:04.867
Its signature move is collections bundling everything, with where filters on every query.

22
00:01:05.167 --> 00:01:09.067
Reach for it when prototyping, demoing, or running a single node.

23
00:01:10.267 --> 00:01:12.433
Its limits are the flip side of its charm.

24
00:01:12.733 --> 00:01:16.433
It's single-node, with modest scale in the millions and coarse tuning.

25
00:01:16.733 --> 00:01:18.900
Production needs more control and scale.

26
00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:23.633
So the standard path is Chroma in dev, and Qdrant or Pinecone in prod.

27
00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:28.633
The very first version of Mission Control RAG was forty lines.

28
00:01:28.933 --> 00:01:33.967
A persistent Chroma client, five hundred report chunks with mission and year metadata.

29
00:01:34.267 --> 00:01:37.633
A query with a where filter, and the results into a prompt.

30
00:01:37.933 --> 00:01:39.240
Built in an evening.

31
00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:44.533
And the restart test, kill Python, reopen, query again, is the prove-it exercise.

32
00:01:45.733 --> 00:01:46.487
Let's recap.

33
00:01:46.487 --> 00:01:49.000
Chroma is an embedded, persistent store.

34
00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:52.667
Collections bundle documents, vectors, and metadata.

35
00:01:52.967 --> 00:01:55.167
Filters are built into every query.

36
00:01:55.467 --> 00:01:59.179
Perfect for prototypes, with a clear graduation path.

37
00:01:59.179 --> 00:02:00.300
Next up: Qdrant.
